*******************************************************************************
* The following .include lines are necessary for all your CSCI 50.02 JSim
* exercises. These work like your Java import statement.
*******************************************************************************

.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"

*******************************************************************************

* The code is similar to the exercise in lab 2 pdf document where instead of using ANDs
* and ORs we used NANDs and inverters to implement the circuit. The circuit is a 3-input function that uses 
* 3 layers of NAND gates to implement the function. The first layer inverts the inputs, the second layer 
* feeds the inverted outputs into a NAND gate, and the final layer outputs the result of the NAND gate.
*******************************************************************************

.subckt fx a b c d e f g out

* Layer 1: Inverted Inputs
Xnand_ab a b w1 nand2
Xnand_cde c d e w2 nand3
Xnand_fg f g w3 nand2

* Layer 2: Feed the inverted outputs into a nand
Xnand_final w1 w2 w3 out nand3

.ends

*******************************************************************************
* The code uses only NANDs and NOTs to implement a full adder circuit. 
* The circuit takes in three inputs (a, b, and cin) and produces two outputs (sum and cout).
* The circuit uses a combination of inverters and NAND gates to produce the desired outputs. 
* The first layer of the circuit inverts the inputs, 
* the second layer feeds the inverted outputs into a NAND gate to produce the sum output,
* and the final layer uses additional NAND gates to produce the carry output.
*
*
* We first used ANDs and ORs and XORs to implement the circuit, 
* then we replaced them with XORs and NANDs since the last part of the circuit 
* is similar to the subcircuit fx.
* We then tested out different combinations such as XORs and NANDs, NANDs and NOTs 
* and NANDs only, with NANDs and NOTs being the most efficient in terms of propagation delay and power consumption.
* While normally XORs should be efficient, because of how jsim and stdcell.jsim implements XORs
* 2-XORs recieve a propagation delay of 150ps while 2-NANDs recieve a propagation delay of 30ps,
* and NOTs receiving 20ps
*
* We didn't really factor in NOR gates since they are not as efficient as NANDs and NOTs,
* and we didn't really use them in our circuit.
* The Circuit has a crictical path of inverter to 3NANDs to 4NANDs where t_pd=20+50+70 which is 140ps
* compared to the XOR circuit which goes to XOR to XOR has a t_pd = 150+150 which is 300ps
***************************************************************************



.subckt fa a b cin sum cout

    Xinva a inva inverter
    Xinvb b invb inverter
    Xinvc cin invc inverter

    Xnand1 inva invb cin nand1 nand3
    Xnand2 inva b invc nand2 nand3
    Xnand3 a invb invc nand3 nand3
    Xnand4 a b cin nand4 nand3

    Xsum nand1 nand2 nand3 nand4 sum nand4

    Xnand5 inva b nand5 nand2
    Xnand6 a invb nand6 nand2
    Xnand7 nand5 nand6 sum1 nand2

    Xcarry1 a b carry1 nand2
    Xcarry2 sum1 cin carry2 nand2
    Xcarry3 carry1 carry2 cout nand2

.ends fa


Xtest_fx clk1 clk2 clk3 clk4 clk5 clk6 clk7 out_fx fx
Xfull_adder1 clk1 clk2 clk3 sum carry fa


.tran 1800ns
.plot clk1
.plot clk2
.plot clk3
.plot clk4
.plot clk5
.plot clk6
.plot clk7
.plot out_fx
.plot sum
.plot carry


*******************************************************************************
